var __index = {"config":{"lang":["en"],"separator":"[\\s\\-]+","pipeline":["stopWordFilter"],"fields":{"title":{"boost":1000.0},"text":{"boost":1.0},"tags":{"boost":1000000.0}}},"docs":[{"location":"index.html","title":"Home","text":"10:42 Meshtastic best-ef-crew alexhappy forest \ud83c\udf33 made it through the gate! samin line at trading post, brb youset up at camp K3, look for the orange flag jesshappy forest fam! 30min out from gate alexsherwood opens at sundown, we heading in? sambattery dying, finding power \ud83d\udd0b youvibe check \u2728 everyone good? jessall good! kandi for days \ud83c\udf08 alexthis set at ranch arena is INSANE \ud83d\udd25 samanyone heading to sherwood? youi\u2019m at carousel club, under the hands of love \ud83c\udfa0 jessdrop a pin, can\u2019t find anyone alexhappy forest! coffee at lucky lake? samwook squad assembling at camp youPLURR vibes \u270c\ud83d\udc9c jessyoga at the meadow in 20 alexheadliner at 9pm, rally at 8? samtrading kandi at the totem \ud83c\udf08 yougiving tree at midnight, who\u2019s in jessim at the observatory \ud83d\udd2d alexthis DJ is unreal, you need to be here samlost my flow toys deep in sherwood \ud83d\ude2d youfound a sherwood shepherd, all good jessrain incoming \u2614 head back? alexhappy forest day 3!! \u2600\ufe0f sambattery at 3%, save my pin youtrading post run for sunscreen jessanyone got pashminas to trade? alexsherwood was MAGIC last night samlineup tonight is stacked yourally at ranch arena 7pm jesssee y\u2019all there \ud83d\udc9c alexsaturday hits different, fam \ud83c\udf33 samhappy forest fam \u2764\ufe0f youcaught sunrise from the dunes, unreal jessback to camp for a nap, regroup at 6? alexdrop a pin if you\u2019re napping, i\u2019ll find you samsunset at the observatory deck? youyes! bring the totem jesson my way, totem squad assemble \ud83d\udc9c\ud83c\udf33 mirajust rolled in! where\u2019s camp? yourow K, third in, orange flag still up finnanyone near the hangar? lost my crew alexheading that way in 10, hold tight samCARLLLLL \ud83d\ude02 youCARRRRL has been spotted at tripolee jesswater refill run, anyone need bottles topped kaiYES bring mine pls, im at the chapel mirathis b2b is melting me \ud83d\ude0d youwhich stage?? coming mirajubilee, back left by the trees finnmesh saved us again, no bars anywhere alexsurprise guest at ranch arena rn, RUN youon the way! leaving sherwood now jessfound a charging tent by main street \ud83d\udd0c samdropping a pin at the observatory deck kaifood truck line on main street is wild rn yougrilled cheese cart by general store, no line mirabless you, on my way \ud83d\ude4f finnjust hugged 3 strangers, forest is healing alextotem squad assemble at giving tree 11pm youbringing the disco ball totem \ud83d\udc83 rileyfirst forest! where\u2019s the trash bar?? jessdeep in sherwood, follow the disco lights rileyfound it!!! omg this place \ud83e\udd2f youwelcome to forest, riley \ud83c\udf33 samweather hold? sky looks sus \u26c8 kairadar shows it passing in 20, hold at camp alexmud at ranch lol boots up youforest weather has range miracamp shower line moving fast btw finnranch arena set is going OFF rn jesslost a wristband, anyone seen one near hangar? rileyturned in one at info, check there! youon my way to grab it, thx riley kaigrand artique has a secret set in 30 samSAY LESS, rallying the crew alexsunrise at the meadow tmrw, who\u2019s in youIN. setting alarms now miraforest family forever \ud83d\udc9c jessneighbor camp brought us pancakes \ud83e\udd5e finntrade you stickers for sunscreen? youcome thru, K3 has spf for days theofinally synced to mesh, hi forest fam alexwelcome theo! we\u2019re at carousel club rileythis is way better than cell, im a convert youtold u! mesh gang for life samthat set was UNREAL, no words jesscarousel selfie incoming, get in here mirarunning, 2 min out kaisunday already?? noooo \ud83d\ude2d youmake it count, see u in sherwood at sundown finnlast sherwood run, who\u2019s coming \ud83c\udfa0 alexall of us. all of us. rileycried twice today and im not even sorry theolove yall. see you next forest \ud83d\udc9c youdrop pins one last time before we scatter jesspinned. giving tree. one more group hug mirahappy forest fam. till the trees light up again \ud83c\udf33\ud83d\udc9c yousee you in the forest \u2728 <p>Real chats from your squad \u2014 no cell tower needed.</p> 400+nodes last year 15,000+messages last year 4days of Forest <p>  Join EF Meshtastic on Discord  New here? Start with How to Connect </p> <p>Not in the Discord? Join at discord.gg/electricforest first.</p>"},{"location":"index.html#find-your-squad-at-electric-forest-without-cell-service","title":"Find Your Squad at Electric Forest \u2014 Without Cell Service.","text":"<p>Meshtastic is a tiny radio that clips to your pack and lets you text your friends across the Forest even when cell service is dead. No subscription. No SIM card. No monthly fee. About $30 to get on the mesh.</p> <p>This is the community guide for using it at Electric Forest.</p>"},{"location":"index.html#what-is-meshtastic","title":"What is Meshtastic?","text":"<p>Meshtastic is free messaging on tiny radios that talk directly to each other \u2014 your message hops phone-to-phone through everyone nearby until it reaches your friend, no cell service needed.</p> <p>Each phone passes the message along to every phone nearby \u2014 so even from the far side of the Forest, your message can hop through the crowd until it reaches your crew.</p>"},{"location":"index.html#get-started-in-3-steps","title":"Get Started in 3 Steps","text":"<ul> <li> <p> 1. Get a radio</p> <p>The easiest pick is the SenseCAP MeshTracker X1 (~$50). It charges over USB-C. See our full picks.</p> <p>Recommended Hardware</p> </li> <li> <p> 2. Configure it for the Forest</p> <p>There are a few specific settings you need to use to be compatible with the EF mesh. Don't get creative \u2014 just follow the steps.</p> <p>How to Connect</p> </li> <li> <p> 3. Find your squad</p> <p>Scan the official EF channels QR, then set up your private squad channel. Then start texting.</p> </li> </ul>"},{"location":"index.html#need-help","title":"Need Help?","text":"<p>The EF Meshtastic crew lives in the official Electric Forest Discord, in the dedicated Meshtastic thread. Setup questions, troubleshooting, \"is my node working?\" \u2014 drop in.</p> <p>  Ask in the EF Discord Meshtastic Thread </p> <p>Not in the Discord? Join at discord.gg/electricforest first.</p>"},{"location":"index.html#why-bring-meshtastic-to-the-forest","title":"Why Bring Meshtastic to the Forest?","text":"<p>Bebop'n around the Forest solo (and a little lost) is part of the magic. But there are moments where you actually need to find your squad \u2014 and cell service in Sherwood is famously spotty.</p> <p>We had real success bringing these little radios to Forest last year. Setup is easier than it looks. And once you have one, you have it for every festival, backpacking trip, and emergency for years.</p> <p>These devices mesh \u2014 every additional radio in the Forest makes the whole network stronger for everyone. Bring one. Bring two. Get your squad on.</p> <p>Heads up \u2014 this is unofficial. This site is run by Forest fam, not by Electric Forest, Insomniac, or Madison House Presents. It's just a friendly guide to help you and your squad stay connected on-grounds. Everything here is community knowledge, shared in good faith.</p>"},{"location":"docs/advanced.html","title":"Advanced Options","text":"<p>For folks who want to roll their own hardware, push more power, or experiment with mesh-monitoring software. Most people don't need this. A MeshTracker X1 and you're set for the Forest.</p>"},{"location":"docs/advanced.html#camp-node-with-wifi-bq-station-g3","title":"Camp Node With WiFi: B&amp;Q Station G3","text":"<p>For people who want a camp node they can reach over WiFi, and who'd rather pick their own antenna and cable. This is a more advanced node than the PeakMesh camp nodes: it's a bare radio, so you handle the power, mounting and antenna yourself.</p> <p></p> In the box The radio and a stock 915 MHz antenna. No USB cable, case or battery. Pick US915MHz when you order. What you need A good outdoor antenna and a short quality cable \u00b7 Power: a USB-C PD supply or power bank that does 15V (25W+), or a 9\u201319V DC barrel plug \u00b7 Something to mount it on, up high Heads up No battery, case, solar or built-in GPS \u00b7 Ships from China and sometimes sells out Guide Meshtastic Station Series docs"},{"location":"docs/advanced.html#bq-station-g3","title":"B&amp;Q Station G3","text":"<p>~$139</p> <ul> <li>More than 1W transmit power (31 dBm by default)</li> <li>WiFi, so you can manage it over your camp hotspot or local network</li> <li>SMA antenna jack, so you can use any antenna and cable you like</li> <li>Modular, with add-on sockets for GPS and sensors</li> </ul> <p>Buy from BQ Voyage </p>"},{"location":"docs/advanced.html#software-for-power-users","title":"Software for Power Users","text":""},{"location":"docs/advanced.html#meshmonitor","title":"MeshMonitor","text":"<p>A self-hosted web tool that auto-acknowledges messages and runs auto-traceroutes. Great for nerds running infrastructure nodes \u2014 typically deployed on a Raspberry Pi at camp. Helps the mesh self-heal by mapping connectivity automatically.</p> <p>If you're comfortable with self-hosted tools and a Pi, check out MeshMonitor.</p>"},{"location":"docs/advanced.html#meshsense","title":"MeshSense","text":"<p>Don't want to mess with a Pi? MeshSense runs on your laptop (Mac/PC/Linux) and does auto-traceroutes that help strengthen the mesh. Easier on-ramp than MeshMonitor.</p> <p>MeshSense by Affirmatech</p>"},{"location":"docs/advanced.html#antennas-deep-dive","title":"Antennas (Deep Dive)","text":"<p>Recommended antennas:</p> <ul> <li>Muzi 17cm Whip \u2014 handheld upgrade, SMA male, ~$12</li> <li>ALFA 915 MHz 5dBi N-Type 7\" Outdoor \u2014 base station omni</li> <li>Rokland 5.8 dBi N-Male Omni Outdoor (large) \u2014 permanent outdoor mounts</li> </ul> <p>Tips:</p> <ul> <li>Watch out for fakes on Amazon \u2014 antennas are the most counterfeited Meshtastic accessory. Buy from the trusted sources above.</li> <li>For base stations, use quality SMA or N-type cable. Keep the cable short \u2014 every foot adds signal loss.</li> <li>More dB \u2260 more coverage. A directional high-dB antenna in the wrong orientation has less useful coverage than a modest omni.</li> </ul> <p></p>"},{"location":"docs/advanced.html#example-setups","title":"Example Setups","text":"<p>Folks in the community have shared their builds. Drop yours in the EF Discord (not in the Discord? join at discord.gg/electricforest first) and we'll feature it here.</p>"},{"location":"docs/advanced.html#cube-totem-mount","title":"Cube Totem Mount","text":"<p>A T-Echo mounted to a 3D-printed bracket with a tripod-cheese-board adapter, riding on top of a Hyper Cube totem. Visible from across camp and high enough to push range significantly.</p> <p></p>"},{"location":"docs/advanced.html#g2-mobile-setup","title":"G2 Mobile Setup","text":"<p>A Station G2 powered via USB-C, with an SMA cable running out to a roof-mounted antenna on a vehicle. Functions as a mobile base station that follows you to and from camp.</p>"},{"location":"docs/advanced.html#test-setup-t-echo-1w-base","title":"Test Setup (T-Echo + 1W Base)","text":"<p>1\u00d7 T-Echo handheld + 1\u00d7 1W base station = easily 1+ mile range hip-height. Dead spots at sharp elevation drops or through dense tree cover. Two-node minimum to start seeing real benefit.</p> <p></p> <p></p>"},{"location":"docs/advanced.html#need-help","title":"Need Help?","text":"<p>  Ask in the EF Discord </p> <p>Not in the Discord? Join at discord.gg/electricforest first.</p>"},{"location":"docs/camp-nodes.html","title":"Setting Up a Camp Node","text":"<p>A camp node is a solar radio you mount up high at camp and leave running all weekend. It helps everyone around you, especially people on small handhelds. Height is might: the higher it goes, the more of the Forest it covers.</p> <p>Leave it on the Client role</p> <p>Even if your node is up high and running all weekend, do not set it to Repeater or Router unless someone in the EF Discord (not in the Discord? join at discord.gg/electricforest first) has told you it's OK. Misconfigured Router or Repeater nodes break the mesh for the whole Forest. The default <code>Client</code> role still helps a ton when it's mounted high.</p>"},{"location":"docs/camp-nodes.html#why-we-only-recommend-1w-or-stronger-camp-nodes","title":"Why We Only Recommend 1W (or Stronger) Camp Nodes","text":"<p>There are lots of solar \"repeater\" and \"base station\" nodes for sale, but most of them use the same small radio as a pocket tracker: about 22 dBm, or roughly 0.16 watts. Mounted up high they still help a little, but not much more than a handheld would.</p> <p>A 1-watt radio (30 dBm) sends about 6 times the power. In the real world that's roughly 2 to 2.5 times the range: more with a clear line of sight, less through trees, RVs and crowds. At the Forest, that's often the difference between reaching the rest of the mesh and not.</p> <p>How to tell when you're shopping: look for \"1W\", \"30 dBm\", \"High Power\" or \"booster\" in the specs. If it says 22 dBm, or doesn't mention power at all, it's a regular-power node.</p> <p>Power isn't everything</p> <p>Extra power helps your node send further. How well it hears other nodes still comes down to antenna and height. A 1W node mounted high on a pole beats a 1W node sitting on a cooler.</p>"},{"location":"docs/camp-nodes.html#our-pick-peakmesh-high-power-solar-node","title":"Our Pick: PeakMesh High-Power Solar Node","text":"<p>PeakMesh makes solar Meshtastic nodes in the US that come assembled and tested. Charge it, screw on the antenna, mount it up high, and leave it for the weekend. The High Power version has a 1-watt radio, so it reaches much further than a regular node.</p> <p>When you order, pick these options</p> <ul> <li>Radio: RAK High Power Kit. Not \"Standard\" and not \"DIY No Radio Module\".</li> <li>Antenna: pick one of the Alfa antennas unless you already have one.</li> <li>Color: whatever you like.</li> </ul> <p>Pick the model that fits how you'll mount it at camp:</p> <ul> <li> <p></p> <p>PeakMesh Ultimate</p> <p>~$180 \u00b7 Pole, wall, or gutter</p> <p>View on Etsy </p> </li> <li> <p></p> <p>PeakMesh Summit</p> <p>~$205 \u00b7 Pole mount, extra power</p> <p>View on Etsy </p> </li> <li> <p></p> <p>PeakMesh Altitude</p> <p>~$185 \u00b7 Hangs from a tree</p> <p>View on Etsy </p> </li> <li> <p></p> <p>PeakMesh Magnet Mover</p> <p>~$185 \u00b7 Magnet, lies flat (car or trailer roof)</p> <p>View on Etsy </p> </li> <li> <p></p> <p>PeakMesh Magnet Climber</p> <p>~$185 \u00b7 Magnet, stands upright</p> <p>View on Etsy </p> </li> </ul> <p>Prices are with the High Power option, from PeakMesh's Etsy shop, and can change. See the full PeakMesh shop.</p>"},{"location":"docs/camp-nodes.html#high-power-sold-out-build-it-yourself","title":"High Power Sold Out? Build It Yourself","text":"<p>If the High Power option is sold out, it's easy to put together yourself:</p> <ol> <li>Order the same PeakMesh model with the \"DIY No Radio Module\" option. That gets you the case, solar panel and battery, ready for a radio.</li> <li>Order the RAK WisMesh High Power Booster Starter Kit (~$39). Choose Battery Powered / 900MHz (US915). The 800MHz version won't work in the US.</li> <li>Put the kit in the case. The modules plug together, and PeakMesh's listing photos show the cable routing.</li> </ol> <p>Always connect the antenna before powering it on</p> <p>Running a radio without its antenna attached can damage it.</p> <p>Stuck? Ask in the Discord. Someone has built one.</p>"},{"location":"docs/camp-nodes.html#mounting-tips","title":"Mounting Tips","text":"<ul> <li>Higher beats everything. A flag pole, a totem, or the top of a tall structure helps far more than any upgrade.</li> <li>Point the solar panel at open sky, away from tent shade.</li> <li>If you use an antenna cable, keep it short. Every foot loses some signal.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"docs/camp-nodes.html#the-observatory-stage-letter","title":"The Observatory Stage Letter","text":"<p>The EF Meshtastic community has drafted an open letter to Electric Forest staff asking permission to host a single high-elevation Meshtastic node at the Observatory Stage. It would act as a backbone repeater with line-of-sight coverage over the whole grounds.</p> <p>It's a community-led ask, not endorsed by EF.</p> <p> Read the letter</p>"},{"location":"docs/camp-nodes.html#want-wifi-or-your-own-antenna","title":"Want WiFi or Your Own Antenna?","text":"<p>If you want a camp node you can reach over WiFi, and you'd rather pick your own antenna and cable, we recommend the B&amp;Q Station G3. It's more advanced: no battery, case or solar, so you handle power and mounting yourself. See Advanced Options.</p>"},{"location":"docs/camp-nodes.html#need-help-with-a-camp-node","title":"Need Help With a Camp Node?","text":"<p>  Ask in the EF Discord </p> <p>Not in the Discord? Join at discord.gg/electricforest first.</p>"},{"location":"docs/how-to-connect.html","title":"How to Connect Your Radio to the EF Mesh","text":"<p>Welcome! This guide walks you through setting up a brand-new Meshtastic radio for Electric Forest. If you've never touched one of these before, you're in the right place. The setup looks like a lot at first \u2014 but you only do it once, and then your radio just works for the entire weekend (and every Forest after).</p> <p>Don't have a radio yet?</p> <p>No worries. Check our Recommended Hardware page \u2014 there are great options starting around $30. Order it now and it'll arrive before you head to Rothbury.</p> <p>Find the nerd in your squad</p> <p>A lot of this setup is \"do it once and share.\" If one person in your crew is a tech nerd, have them do the QR code parts (Step 4 and Step 9) and the rest of you just scan their QR. Easy.</p>"},{"location":"docs/how-to-connect.html#step-1-update-your-firmware","title":"Step 1: Update Your Firmware","text":"<p>This is honestly the most confusing part of Meshtastic, so we get it out of the way first. An up-to-date radio works better, ships with better defaults, and avoids weird bugs.</p> <ol> <li>Plug your radio into your laptop with a USB-C cable.</li> <li>Open Meshtastic Web Flasher in Google Chrome (Firefox and Safari don't support the USB-flashing API).</li> <li>Pick your device from the list, click Flash latest, and follow the on-screen instructions.</li> <li>If your device needs to be in DFU mode to flash, Google \"[your device name] DFU mode\" \u2014 it's usually a button-hold while plugging in.</li> </ol> <p>Your node identity can get wiped</p> <p>Updating firmware sometimes resets your radio's identity (the encryption value shown under Settings \u2192 Security). If after you update your friends say \"we can see each other but not you,\" that's the cause. Have them each go to the Nodes tab in the Meshtastic app, tap your node, and tap Delete node. They'll re-fetch your new key automatically. Easy fix.</p> <p>To avoid this entirely: before updating, go to Settings \u2192 Security in the Meshtastic app and screenshot the value shown there. Save the screenshot to your notes app. If it gets wiped, just paste the saved value back in.</p>"},{"location":"docs/how-to-connect.html#step-2-get-the-app-on-your-phone","title":"Step 2: Get the App on Your Phone","text":"<p>Your radio talks to your phone over Bluetooth. You'll do most of the configuring from the phone.</p> Platform Link iPhone Apple App Store Android Google Play Store Web (laptop) client.meshtastic.org \u2014 backup option <p>iOS and Android both work great. Just download the official Meshtastic app.</p>"},{"location":"docs/how-to-connect.html#step-3-pair-your-radio-to-your-phone","title":"Step 3: Pair Your Radio to Your Phone","text":"<ol> <li>Turn on your radio.</li> <li>Open the Meshtastic app.</li> <li>Tap the + button (add device).</li> <li>Your radio should pop up in the list \u2014 tap to pair.</li> <li>If it asks for a pairing PIN, check the documentation that came with your device or the manufacturer's site.</li> </ol> <p>You should now see your node in the app. If not, restart the radio and try again.</p>"},{"location":"docs/how-to-connect.html#step-4-scan-the-official-ef-channels-qr","title":"Step 4: Scan the Official EF Channels QR","text":"<p>This is the easy way to load all the Forest-compatible settings to your radio at once. Only one person in your squad needs to do this \u2014 they'll then generate a new QR and share it with the rest of you.</p> <p>Click here to load EF channels onto your radio </p> <p></p> <p>Or scan this from a second phone to bring it into the mesh.</p> <p>Open this link on the phone that's paired to your radio. It loads four channels \u2014 the Meshtastic default primary channel (left unnamed on purpose \u2014 see the note below), a <code>chgme-squad</code> placeholder for your squad (customize in Step 8), the public <code>forest-chat</code>, and <code>Weather</code> \u2014 all on the Medium Range - Fast preset.</p> <p>QR code not working? Enter the channels manually</p> <p>If the QR or link won't load, you can add each channel by hand in the Meshtastic app (Channels \u2192 +). Enter the name and paste the matching key below exactly as shown.</p> Channel Name Key Notes Primary (channel 0) leave the name blank <code>AQ==</code> The default Meshtastic public channel. See the note below about naming. chgme-squad <code>chgme-squad</code> (then rename to your crew) TBD Your squad's private channel which you need to generate the key for forest-chat <code>forest-chat</code> <code>oU03W9b8s0vpL0IjhBIIHZtuW/sHui3QDhmlaOLflkk=</code> Community forest chat channel Weather <code>Weather</code> <code>Ww==</code> Weather updates channel <p>Channel names must match exactly</p> <p>The channel name \u2014 including capitalization, spelling, and punctuation is just as important as the key. <code>forest-chat</code> and <code>Forest-Chat</code> are not the same channel. Two channels with different names will not see each other, even if they share the same key. If anything is even slightly off, you won't be on the same channel as everyone else. Type the names exactly as shown above.</p> <p>Naming the primary channel: leave it BLANK</p> <p>For the primary channel (channel 0), the right move is to leave the name field blank \u2014 exactly like the QR does. A blank primary is what everyone interoperates on.</p> <p>Here's the part that trips people up. When the name is blank, the two apps display a label for you, and they don't agree:</p> <ul> <li>iOS shows it as \"Primary channel\"</li> <li>Android shows it as \"MediumFast\"</li> </ul> <p>Those are just display labels for the same blank/default channel \u2014 they interoperate fine. The blank name is what matters, not the label your phone draws.</p> <ul> <li>\u2705 Recommended: leave the name blank.</li> <li>\u2705 If you insist on typing a name, type exactly <code>MediumFast</code> (no space, exact capitalization) \u2014 this is what Android shows, so it stays compatible.</li> <li>\u274c Do NOT type <code>Primary</code> or <code>Primary Channel</code>. Those are not the channel name \u2014 they're just what iOS draws on top of a blank one. Typing them gives your channel a real, non-blank name that will not connect to the rest of the Forest mesh.</li> </ul> <p>Stuck? Ask in Discord</p> <p>Post in the EF Meshtastic Discord thread (not in the Discord? join at discord.gg/electricforest first) \u2014 someone in the community is usually around and happy to walk you through it on a quick voice chat.</p>"},{"location":"docs/how-to-connect.html#step-5-set-your-radio-settings","title":"Step 5: Set Your Radio Settings","text":"<p>Open the Meshtastic app \u2192 tap your node \u2192 Radio Configuration. There are a lot of settings here. Please don't get creative \u2014 changing the defaults will either make your radio incompatible with the Forest mesh or flood the network with traffic.</p> <p>Here's exactly what to change. Anything not mentioned, leave alone.</p>"},{"location":"docs/how-to-connect.html#lora","title":"LoRa","text":"<ul> <li>Region: <code>United States</code> \u2190 change this if it's not already set</li> <li>Set Preset: <code>Medium Range - Fast</code> \u2190 this is not the default. Change it from <code>Long Range - Fast</code> to <code>Medium Range - Fast</code>. Why: the Forest is a dense crowd \u2014 hundreds of nodes packed into a few square miles. Medium Fast trades a little range for faster message delivery, lower latency, and way less channel congestion. Better network for everyone when we're all on top of each other.</li> <li>Transmit Enabled: <code>On</code> (default \u2014 leave it)</li> <li>Number of Hops: <code>3</code> (default \u2014 leave it)</li> <li>Frequency Slot: <code>0</code> (default \u2014 leave it)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"docs/how-to-connect.html#user","title":"User","text":"<ul> <li>Long Name: Your festival name / handle</li> <li>Short Name: 3\u20134 characters max (this is what people will see on their map \u2014 keep it tasteful)</li> </ul>"},{"location":"docs/how-to-connect.html#device","title":"Device","text":"<ul> <li>Device Role: <code>Client</code> (leave it)</li> <li>Rebroadcast Mode: <code>ALL</code> (leave it)</li> </ul> <p>Do NOT set your role to Router or Repeater</p> <p>This is the #1 way to break the Forest mesh for everyone. Stick to <code>Client</code>. If you've got an awesome setup at camp and you want to help carry the network, message the EF Discord (not in the Discord? join at discord.gg/electricforest first) before changing this.</p>"},{"location":"docs/how-to-connect.html#position","title":"Position","text":"<ul> <li>Broadcast Interval: <code>1 hour</code></li> <li>Smart Position: <code>ON</code></li> <li>Smart Minimum Interval: <code>30 seconds</code></li> <li>Smart Minimum Distance: <code>100 meters</code></li> <li>Device GPS: <code>Enabled</code></li> <li>GPS Update Interval: <code>30 seconds</code></li> <li>Position Flags: Turn them all off</li> </ul> <p>No GPS on your radio?</p> <p>If your radio doesn't have built-in GPS (or it's flaky), you can use your phone's GPS instead. In the Meshtastic app: App Settings \u2192 Phone GPS sharing \u2192 Enabled. Make sure your phone's location permission for Meshtastic is set to Always.</p>"},{"location":"docs/how-to-connect.html#store-forward","title":"Store &amp; Forward","text":"<ul> <li>DO NOT ENABLE THIS. Just leave it off.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"docs/how-to-connect.html#external-notifications-optional-quiet-your-radio","title":"External Notifications (optional \u2014 quiet your radio)","text":"<p>Hate the beeps and buzzes? Turn these off:</p> <ul> <li>Alert GPIO buzzer when receiving a bell: <code>OFF</code></li> <li>Alert GPIO vibra motor when receiving a bell: <code>OFF</code></li> <li>Alert GPIO buzzer when receiving a message: <code>OFF</code></li> <li>Alert GPIO vibra motor when receiving a message: <code>OFF</code></li> </ul>"},{"location":"docs/how-to-connect.html#step-6-save-and-reboot","title":"Step 6: Save and Reboot","text":"<p>Hit Save in the app. Your radio will reboot every time you save. Wait for it to reconnect (top-right indicator turns green) before you do the next step. Always confirm the settings stuck.</p>"},{"location":"docs/how-to-connect.html#step-7-check-your-channels","title":"Step 7: Check Your Channels","text":"<p>You should now see four channels in the Channels tab:</p> <ol> <li>The primary channel (channel 0) \u2014 leave it alone, this is the default Meshtastic channel. It has no name set, so your phone shows you an auto-generated label: iOS displays \"Primary channel\", Android displays \"MediumFast\". Both are the same blank/default channel and they interoperate fine \u2014 don't \"fix\" the label by typing a name.</li> <li>chgme-squad \u2014 placeholder for your squad (you'll customize in Step 8)</li> <li>forest-chat \u2014 public EF community chat \u2014 leave it alone</li> <li>Weather \u2014 posts the current weather every hour and a forecast at the beginning of every day. Major weather warnings go to the primary channel.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"docs/how-to-connect.html#step-8-set-up-your-squads-encrypted-channel","title":"Step 8: Set Up Your Squad's Encrypted Channel","text":"<p>Tap chgme-squad. This is the channel for just you and your friends.</p> <ol> <li>Change the Name to something unique to your crew \u2014 like <code>disco-ducks</code> or <code>taco-tuesday</code>.</li> <li>Tap the blue lock icon to generate a fresh channel key. This is critical \u2014 it gives your squad channel a unique encryption identifier so other Forest folks can't snoop on your group chat.</li> <li>Enable Allow Position Requests (so your squad can ping for your location).</li> <li>Enable Precise Location (so location shares are accurate to meters, not hundreds of meters).</li> <li>Leave MQTT uplink/downlink disabled.</li> </ol> <p>Hit Save. Wait for the radio to reboot and reconnect.</p>"},{"location":"docs/how-to-connect.html#step-9-share-with-your-squad","title":"Step 9: Share With Your Squad","text":"<p>In the Meshtastic app: Settings \u2192 Share Channel QR Code.</p> <ul> <li>Make sure all 4 channels are selected (Primary, your squad channel, forest-chat, Weather).</li> <li>Double-check your squad channel name is right.</li> <li>Click Share QR Code and send the QR (or link) to everyone in your crew.</li> </ul> <p>Each squad member opens the link/QR on their paired phone \u2014 and boom, they're in.</p>"},{"location":"docs/how-to-connect.html#step-10-test-it","title":"Step 10: Test It","text":"<p>Once everyone's scanned, you all have:</p> <ul> <li>The standard EF primary channel (for hearing everyone)</li> <li>An encrypted channel just for your squad</li> <li>The public <code>forest-chat</code> for the wider Forest community</li> </ul> <p>Send a \"hello world\" message in the squad channel. If you all see it, you're golden.</p> <p>Test it at home BEFORE Forest</p> <p>Seriously. Don't wait until you're in Rothbury to figure this out. Get your squad together at someone's house, do the setup, drive around your neighborhood. The Meshtastic learning curve is real but it's small \u2014 get past it in the comfort of wifi.</p>"},{"location":"docs/how-to-connect.html#what-now","title":"What Now?","text":"<ul> <li>Recommended Settings \u2014 deeper dive on broadcast intervals, GPS, and getting the most out of your node</li> <li>Camp Nodes \u2014 want to help build the mesh? Set up a high-altitude node at your camp</li> <li>Troubleshooting \u2014 common gotchas and fixes</li> </ul>"},{"location":"docs/how-to-connect.html#stuck-weve-got-you","title":"Stuck? We've Got You.","text":"<p>The EF Meshtastic crew lives in the official Electric Forest Discord. We're friendly, we love getting more people on the mesh, and there are no dumb questions.</p> <p>  Get Help in the EF Discord </p> <p>Not in the Discord? Join at discord.gg/electricforest first.</p>"},{"location":"docs/recommended-hardware.html","title":"Recommended Hardware for the Forest","text":"<p>You only need one radio to get on the mesh. Charge it, pair it with your phone, and you're set.</p>"},{"location":"docs/recommended-hardware.html#our-pick","title":"Our Pick","text":"<p>Ships from overseas and sometimes sells out, so order early.</p> <p>Can't get an X1 in time? The RAK WisMesh Tag (~$30\u201350) and SenseCAP T-1000-E (~$40\u201360, make sure it says T-1000-E) work just as well and are often cheaper or easier to find last-minute, including on Amazon. The catch: they charge with their own magnetic cable instead of USB-C, so pack it and don't lose it.</p>"},{"location":"docs/recommended-hardware.html#sensecap-meshtracker-x1","title":"SenseCAP MeshTracker X1","text":"<p>~$50</p> <p>Card-sized, clips to your pack, works out of the box. There's no screen: you read and send messages in the Meshtastic app on your phone.</p> <ul> <li>Charges with a normal USB-C cable, so any phone charger or battery pack works</li> <li>Waterproof (IP66), up to ~5 days of battery</li> <li>Built-in GPS so your squad can see where you are</li> </ul> <p>Buy from Seeed Studio </p>"},{"location":"docs/recommended-hardware.html#buying-tips","title":"Buying Tips","text":"<ul> <li>Order early. Stock sells out and overseas shipping can take 2\u20134 weeks. You'll also want time to set it up and test it before Forest.</li> <li>US sellers ship faster. Rokland and Atlavox keep stock in the US. Overseas stores can be cheaper, but they're slower and can add customs fees.</li> <li>On Amazon, check the seller. Make sure it's the exact model above, sold by a real store.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"docs/recommended-hardware.html#want-to-boost-the-mesh-for-everyone","title":"Want to Boost the Mesh for Everyone?","text":"<p>A solar node mounted up high at your camp helps everyone nearby, especially people on small handhelds.</p> <p>See the Camp Nodes guide </p>"},{"location":"docs/recommended-hardware.html#need-help-picking","title":"Need Help Picking?","text":"<p>Drop a message in the EF Discord Meshtastic thread. We're happy to help.</p> <p>  Ask the EF Discord </p> <p>Not in the Discord? Join at discord.gg/electricforest first.</p>"},{"location":"docs/recommended-settings.html","title":"Recommended Settings for the EF Mesh","text":"<p>These are the settings the EF Meshtastic community uses. They keep the network healthy when hundreds of folks pile into the Forest at once. Follow them and you'll be a good mesh citizen.</p> <p>If you haven't done first-time setup yet</p> <p>Start with How to Connect \u2014 it walks you through the whole 10-step flow. This page is the deeper dive for folks who want to understand why the settings are what they are.</p>"},{"location":"docs/recommended-settings.html#the-forest-channel-bundle-shared-openly","title":"The Forest Channel Bundle (Shared Openly)","text":"<p>Unlike some Meshtastic communities, the EF community shares its channel settings openly. Anyone with this QR can load the Forest channels onto their radio.</p> <p>Load EF Channels </p> <p></p> <p>Or scan this from a second phone to bring it into the mesh.</p> <p>This loads four channels on the Medium Range - Fast preset: the Meshtastic default primary channel (left unnamed \u2014 see the naming note below), a <code>chgme-squad</code> placeholder for your squad (you'll customize), the public <code>forest-chat</code>, and the <code>Weather</code> channel.</p>"},{"location":"docs/recommended-settings.html#channel-keys","title":"Channel Keys","text":"<p>These are the PSKs (pre-shared keys) for each EF Mesh channel. If you scanned the QR or tapped the link above, your device should already have these loaded \u2014 but you can verify them in Settings \u2192 Channels \u2192 [channel] \u2192 Encryption Key.</p> Channel Name Key 0 (blank \u2014 Meshtastic default; see naming note) <code>AQ==</code> 1 Your Squad generate your own 2 forest-chat <code>oU03W9b8s0vpL0IjhBIIHZtuW/sHui3QDhmlaOLflkk=</code> 3 Weather <code>Ww==</code> <p>These are channel PSKs, not device keys \u2014 they're already embedded in the public QR / Meshtastic share link above, so seeing them here doesn't reveal anything that isn't already shared with anyone who joins the mesh. The Squad channel is the one you keep unique to your crew.</p>"},{"location":"docs/recommended-settings.html#lora-settings","title":"LoRa Settings","text":"Setting Value Why Region <code>United States</code> Meshtastic uses 915 MHz in the US \u2014 required for legal operation Preset <code>Medium Range - Fast</code> Change this from the default. The Forest is a dense crowd \u2014 hundreds of nodes in close proximity. Medium Fast gives faster message delivery, lower latency for quick-coordination pings, and way less channel congestion than Long Fast. We trade a bit of range (which we don't really need at festival distances) for a much healthier network when everyone's piled into Sherwood. Number of Hops <code>3</code> Default. More hops = more network flood. Less = shorter reach. 3 is the sweet spot. Frequency Slot <code>0</code> Default. Everyone needs to be on the same slot. Transmit Enabled <code>On</code> Default. If this is off, you can't send anything."},{"location":"docs/recommended-settings.html#channels","title":"Channels","text":""},{"location":"docs/recommended-settings.html#channel-0-primary","title":"Channel 0 \u2014 Primary","text":"Setting Value Notes Name (leave blank) This is the Meshtastic-wide default channel. Leave the name field empty \u2014 see the naming note below. Key <code>AQ==</code> Default Meshtastic key, leave as-is Channel Role <code>Primary</code> Don't change. (This is the channel role field, not the name \u2014 it's fine that it says \"Primary\" here.) Position <code>Disabled</code> Important \u2014 see note below MQTT Uplink / Downlink <code>Disabled</code> Don't change <p>Encryption Key: <code>AQ==</code></p> <p>Leave the primary channel's NAME blank \u2014 don't type 'Primary'</p> <p>Channels with different names will not see each other, even on the same key. The compatible primary is the one with a blank name (that's exactly what the EF QR loads).</p> <p>With a blank name, each app auto-displays a label and they don't match: iOS shows \"Primary channel\", Android shows \"MediumFast\". Same blank/default channel, different label \u2014 they interoperate fine.</p> <ul> <li>\u2705 Recommended: leave the name blank.</li> <li>\u2705 If you must type a name, type exactly <code>MediumFast</code> (no space, exact caps) \u2014 matches Android's label and stays compatible.</li> <li>\u274c Do not type <code>Primary</code> or <code>Primary Channel</code> \u2014 those are just iOS's display label, not the channel name. Typing them gives the channel a real name that won't connect to the Forest mesh.</li> </ul> <p>Why turn off position on Channel 0?</p> <p>On Meshtastic firmware 2.7+, your radio shares your GPS position from whatever channel is first in the list. By disabling position on Channel 0 (the public default), your precise location only goes to your squad channel \u2014 not the world.</p> <p>This only works on firmware 2.7 or later. Make sure you updated in Step 1.</p>"},{"location":"docs/recommended-settings.html#channel-1-your-squad","title":"Channel 1 \u2014 Your Squad","text":"Setting Value Notes Name (your squad name) Customize in Step 8 Key (unique \u2014 tap blue lock to generate) CRITICAL \u2014 tap the blue lock to generate a unique key. Don't skip this. Channel Role <code>Secondary</code> Don't change Allow Position Requests <code>Enabled</code> So your squad can ping your location Precise Location <code>Enabled</code> So squad pings give meters, not hundreds of meters MQTT Uplink / Downlink <code>Disabled</code> Don't change"},{"location":"docs/recommended-settings.html#channel-2-forest-chat-public-ef-community","title":"Channel 2 \u2014 forest-chat (public EF community)","text":"Setting Value Notes Name <code>forest-chat</code> Comes preset via the QR Key <code>oU03W9b8s0vpL0IjhBIIHZtuW/sHui3QDhmlaOLflkk=</code> Comes preset via the QR Channel Role <code>Secondary</code> Don't change Allow Position Requests <code>Enabled</code> Optional \u2014 enable if you want broader Forest folks to see your location Precise Location <code>Enabled</code> Optional MQTT Uplink / Downlink <code>Disabled</code> Don't change <p>Encryption Key: <code>oU03W9b8s0vpL0IjhBIIHZtuW/sHui3QDhmlaOLflkk=</code></p>"},{"location":"docs/recommended-settings.html#channel-3-weather","title":"Channel 3 \u2014 Weather","text":"Setting Value Notes Name <code>Weather</code> Comes preset via the QR Key <code>Ww==</code> Comes preset via the QR Channel Role <code>Secondary</code> Don't change Allow Position Requests <code>Enabled</code> Optional \u2014 enable if you want broader Forest folks to see your location Precise Location <code>Enabled</code> Optional MQTT Uplink / Downlink <code>Disabled</code> Don't change <p>Encryption Key: <code>Ww==</code></p> <p>Posts the current weather every hour and a forecast at the beginning of every day. Major weather warnings go to the Primary channel.</p>"},{"location":"docs/recommended-settings.html#device","title":"Device","text":"Setting Value Why Device Role <code>Client</code> DO NOT CHANGE. Setting Router or Repeater without coordination breaks the mesh for everyone. Rebroadcast Mode <code>ALL</code> Default. Helps relay messages across the mesh. <p>About Router and Repeater roles</p> <p>Unless someone in the EF Discord has explicitly given you the green light, stick to Client. Mobile Router/Repeater nodes cause routing chaos, congest the network, and make everyone's experience worse \u2014 even (especially) yours. If you've got an awesome high-elevation node at your camp and think it'd benefit the community as a repeater, message the EF Discord first (not in the Discord? join at discord.gg/electricforest before tapping the link).</p>"},{"location":"docs/recommended-settings.html#position","title":"Position","text":"<p>For mobile radios (what you'll be wearing at Forest):</p> Setting Value Broadcast Interval <code>1 hour</code> Smart Position <code>ON</code> Smart Minimum Interval <code>30 seconds</code> Smart Minimum Distance <code>100 meters</code> Device GPS <code>Enabled</code> GPS Update Interval <code>30 seconds</code> Position Flags All OFF <p>No built-in GPS?</p> <p>If your radio doesn't have GPS (some don't), use your phone's GPS. In the Meshtastic app: App Settings \u2192 Phone GPS Sharing \u2192 Enabled, and set your phone's location permission for Meshtastic to Always.</p>"},{"location":"docs/recommended-settings.html#store-forward","title":"Store &amp; Forward","text":"<p>Leave this disabled. Always. On every node. Store &amp; Forward floods the network with replays of old messages and is not appropriate for a festival mesh.</p>"},{"location":"docs/recommended-settings.html#external-notifications-quiet-your-radio","title":"External Notifications (Quiet Your Radio)","text":"<p>Most radios make beeps and vibrations every time something happens on the mesh. At Forest with hundreds of nodes, this gets annoying fast. Turn these off:</p> <ul> <li>Alert GPIO buzzer when receiving a bell: <code>OFF</code></li> <li>Alert GPIO vibra motor when receiving a bell: <code>OFF</code></li> <li>Alert GPIO buzzer when receiving a message: <code>OFF</code></li> <li>Alert GPIO vibra motor when receiving a message: <code>OFF</code></li> </ul> <p>You can also turn off the new-node-discovery notification in your phone's notification settings.</p>"},{"location":"docs/recommended-settings.html#mqtt-skip-this-for-now","title":"MQTT (Skip This for Now)","text":"<p>MQTT lets nodes upload metadata to a shared cloud server. We don't have a dedicated EF MQTT bridge set up for 2026 yet, so leave MQTT disabled on every channel \u2014 uplink off, downlink off.</p> <p>If we stand one up before the festival, we'll update this page and post in the EF Discord (not in the Discord? join at discord.gg/electricforest first).</p>"},{"location":"docs/recommended-settings.html#need-help","title":"Need Help?","text":"<p>The EF Meshtastic crew lives in the official Electric Forest Discord \u2014 we love walking new folks through settings.</p> <p>  Ask in the EF Discord </p> <p>Not in the Discord? Join at discord.gg/electricforest first.</p>"},{"location":"docs/troubleshooting.html","title":"Troubleshooting","text":"<p>Common gotchas and fixes. Still stuck after this? Drop in the EF Discord (not in the Discord? join at discord.gg/electricforest first) \u2014 we're happy to help.</p>"},{"location":"docs/troubleshooting.html#my-friends-can-see-each-others-messages-and-i-see-theirs-but-they-cant-see-mine","title":"\"My friends can see each other's messages and I see theirs \u2014 but they can't see mine.\"","text":"<p>This is the most common Meshtastic issue, and it's almost always the same cause: your node identity got reset (usually after a firmware update).</p> <p>Every Meshtastic node has a unique identity stored in Settings \u2192 Security. Your friends' radios remember this identity and use it to decrypt your messages. If yours changes (because you updated firmware or factory-reset), they're trying to decrypt with the old one.</p> <p>Fix: Have everyone in your squad open the Meshtastic app, tap the Nodes tab, find your node, and tap Delete node. Their radios will re-fetch your new identity automatically. Done.</p> <p>To prevent it next time:</p> <ol> <li>Open the Meshtastic app.</li> <li>Settings \u2192 Security.</li> <li>Screenshot the encryption value shown there. Save it to your phone's notes app.</li> <li>If a firmware update wipes it, paste the saved value back in.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"docs/troubleshooting.html#im-not-seeing-many-nodes-or-messages","title":"\"I'm not seeing many nodes or messages.\"","text":"<p>A few things to check:</p> <ul> <li>Get higher. Walls, backpacks, even bodies attenuate the signal. Hold your node up high.</li> <li>Give it time. The mesh takes a few minutes to \"settle\" and find optimal paths. Be patient on a fresh setup.</li> <li>Recheck your settings. Make sure they actually saved \u2014 your radio might have been disconnected from the app when you hit Save. Confirm by reopening the settings.</li> <li>Drive around. If you're testing at home, drive a few blocks and see what nodes pop up. Stationary at home you might just be in a dead spot.</li> <li>Get a second node. Two-node testing is the minimum to actually verify your setup. Get a cheap second node (a WisMesh Tag or T-1000-E works) and have a friend take it for a walk.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"docs/troubleshooting.html#my-radio-wont-connect-to-my-phone","title":"\"My radio won't connect to my phone.\"","text":"<ol> <li>Force quit and reopen the Meshtastic app.</li> <li>Toggle Bluetooth off and on on your phone.</li> <li>Restart your radio. Hold the power button to reboot.</li> <li>Forget and re-pair. In your phone's Bluetooth settings, forget the Meshtastic device, then re-pair from the app.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"docs/troubleshooting.html#i-changed-a-setting-and-now-everything-is-broken","title":"\"I changed a setting and now everything is broken.\"","text":"<p>The most common breakage points:</p> <ul> <li>Device Role changed to anything other than Client. Set it back to <code>Client</code>. Save. Wait for reboot.</li> <li>Region changed off United States. Set it back to <code>United States</code>. (Required for legal 915 MHz operation in the US.)</li> <li>Frequency Slot changed from 0. Set it back to <code>0</code>.</li> <li>Preset changed from Medium Range - Fast. Set it back to <code>Medium Range - Fast</code>. (Note: the Meshtastic default is <code>Long Range - Fast</code> \u2014 the Forest community runs Medium Fast instead because we're packed close together. See Recommended Settings for the reasoning.)</li> </ul> <p>If you really borked things, factory-reset the radio (long-press button or via the app), then re-scan the EF channels QR from scratch. Five-minute fix \u2014 preset is baked into the QR, so no manual flip needed.</p> <p></p> <p>Scan this from a second phone if you've already nuked the link on yours.</p>"},{"location":"docs/troubleshooting.html#my-battery-is-dying-way-faster-than-i-expected","title":"\"My battery is dying way faster than I expected.\"","text":"<ul> <li>Turn off device buzzers and vibrations in External Notifications (see Recommended Settings).</li> <li>Increase the Position Broadcast Interval to 1 hour or longer.</li> <li>Disable Device GPS and use your phone's GPS instead (saves significant battery).</li> <li>Lower screen brightness if your radio has a screen.</li> <li>Carry a power bank. Festival days are long.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"docs/troubleshooting.html#where-are-the-other-forest-folks","title":"\"Where are the other Forest folks?\"","text":"<p>Mesh propagation depends on density. Before the festival kicks off, the mesh is sparse. As more people arrive and turn on radios, the network grows organically. Don't panic if Wednesday afternoon is quiet \u2014 by Friday night it'll be lively.</p> <p>Drop into the EF Discord Meshtastic thread (not in the Discord? join at discord.gg/electricforest first) to find folks who are already on-grounds.</p>"},{"location":"docs/troubleshooting.html#my-gps-isnt-getting-a-fix","title":"\"My GPS isn't getting a fix.\"","text":"<ul> <li>Get outside, away from buildings/trees. GPS needs sky.</li> <li>Be patient on cold start. First fix can take 5\u201315 minutes on a fresh radio.</li> <li>Check that GPS is enabled in Position settings (see Recommended Settings).</li> <li>Fall back to phone GPS. If your radio's GPS is unreliable, enable Phone GPS Sharing in the Meshtastic app. Set your phone's location permission for Meshtastic to Always.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"docs/troubleshooting.html#more-resources","title":"More Resources","text":"<ul> <li>Meshtastic official docs \u2014 Introduction \u2014 the canonical Meshtastic reference</li> <li>Best Meshtastic Nodes of 2025 \u2014 Ham Radio Crash Course \u2014 great hardware comparison video</li> <li>Find your local Meshtastic community \u2014 test your setup with locals before Forest</li> </ul>"},{"location":"docs/troubleshooting.html#still-stuck","title":"Still Stuck?","text":"<p>  Ask in the EF Discord </p> <p>Not in the Discord? Join at discord.gg/electricforest first.</p>"},{"location":"docs/wish-machine-request.html","title":"Wish Machine Request \u2014 Letter to Electric Forest","text":"<p>This is a community-led open letter from Forest attendees to the staff of Electric Forest. We're asking for permission and support to expand Meshtastic infrastructure on-grounds \u2014 specifically, a single high-elevation repeater node at the Observatory Stage that would provide line-of-sight coverage across the entire Forest site.</p> <p>This letter is not affiliated with or endorsed by Electric Forest, Insomniac, or Madison House Presents. It is sent on behalf of attendees organized through the EF Meshtastic Discord thread (not in the Discord? join at discord.gg/electricforest first) and represents a community request \u2014 not an official partnership.</p>"},{"location":"docs/wish-machine-request.html#the-letter","title":"The Letter","text":"<p>To the Kind and Visionary Staff of Electric Forest,</p> <p>We are writing to you today with a simple, yet powerful proposal to significantly enhance the safety and communication experience for all Forest attendees: the installation of a single, dedicated Meshtastic LoRa node atop the Observatory Stage.</p> <p>As you know, reliable cell service can be spotty in the Forest. Our community has begun embracing Meshtastic, a mesh radio communication network that allows attendees to send text messages and GPS coordinates without relying on cell towers. This initiative is entirely community-driven and aims to solve a fundamental Forest problem: finding your squad when the music is loud and the signal is out.</p> <p>The success of this project hinges on network coverage. Our current, user-based network is a great start, but to truly become a reliable tool for the entire community \u2014 helping friends reconnect, coordinate meetups, and even assist with lost and found \u2014 we need a central, high-elevation point.</p> <p>The Observatory Stage is the ideal location. Its height would provide Line of Sight (LOS) across the entire Electric Forest site. Placing a single router/node here would act as a powerful backbone, instantly expanding the network's reach and stability to cover the vast majority of the grounds. This single act would transform Meshtastic from a niche project into a robust, site-wide communication layer.</p> <p>This small contribution would represent a massive success for the community, demonstrating a commitment to supporting independent, innovative solutions that directly benefit the Forest family.</p> <p>We are ready to partner with you on the technical details. Please consider this request and help us find a kind-hearted EF employee who can assist us in placing a community Meshtastic node on the Observatory Stage.</p> <p>Thank you for your time and for continuing to make Electric Forest an amazing place.</p> <p>Sincerely,</p> <p>The Electric Forest Meshtastic Community</p>"},{"location":"docs/wish-machine-request.html#why-the-observatory-stage","title":"Why the Observatory Stage?","text":"<p>A handful of practical reasons the Observatory was chosen as the ask:</p> <ul> <li>Elevation. The single biggest factor in Meshtastic range is line-of-sight. Height beats wattage. The Observatory's elevation would put a node above the tree canopy that blocks most ground-level traffic in Sherwood.</li> <li>One node, massive impact. Adding more low-elevation camp nodes helps the mesh incrementally. A single high-elevation backbone node helps the mesh exponentially.</li> <li>Low operational footprint. Solar-powered nodes like the PeakMesh high-power solar radios don't need power runs, network drops, or maintenance during the weekend. Mount it, leave it, retrieve it Sunday night.</li> <li>Festival-friendly. Meshtastic is text-only, low-power, and uses unlicensed ISM-band radio (the same 915 MHz band as cordless phones and baby monitors). It doesn't interfere with stage gear, comms, or licensed festival operations.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"docs/wish-machine-request.html#what-were-asking-for","title":"What We're Asking For","text":"<p>In short:</p> <ol> <li>Permission to mount one solar-powered Meshtastic repeater node at the Observatory Stage for the duration of the festival.</li> <li>Access to a kind-hearted EF staffer who can help with the placement and any logistics.</li> <li>A festival-friendly policy going forward for community-installed mesh infrastructure at expanded locations (e.g., Sherwood Court).</li> </ol> <p>We bring our own hardware. We handle setup and tear-down. We follow whatever placement guidance EF gives us. We just need a yes.</p>"},{"location":"docs/wish-machine-request.html#want-to-help","title":"Want to Help?","text":"<ul> <li>Bring a camp node. Every node added to the network strengthens it. See the Camp Nodes page for pre-built options.</li> <li>Spread the word. Share this page with your Forest friends.</li> </ul> <p>  Join the EF Meshtastic Discord  Camp Nodes Guide  Back to Home </p> <p>Not in the Discord? Join at discord.gg/electricforest first.</p>"}]}